<!--
  balancer.xml — a planar ankle-and-hip balancer, written for Synapsa Commons lesson F15-L08
  (sim-to-real, and what a free tier cannot teach you).

  Authored for this lesson by the Synapsa Commons project and released under CC0-1.0
  (https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/). Nothing is downloaded: the file
  ships inside the lesson directory and the lesson never touches the network.

  WHY THIS MODEL

  The lesson's subject is the reality gap, not locomotion. It has to run four separate
  studies — control latency, actuator saturation, sensor noise and domain randomisation —
  and each one needs hundreds of independent rollouts to say anything with a straight face.
  So the model is the smallest machine that still has a humanoid's defining problem: it is
  an inverted pendulum that falls over unless something actively holds it up.

  Contacts are disabled and the ankle is a pin joint rather than a foot on a floor. That is
  a deliberate, declared simplification — it is itself one of the reality gaps the lesson
  measures, and it is named as such in the notebook. The payoff is a contact-free mj_step
  costing a couple of microseconds, which is what lets a student run a full randomised
  study inside a CPU budget instead of reading about one.

  GEOMETRY

  Two links in the x-z plane, both hinging about the y axis:

      ankle  hinge at the origin     shank rotates about the ground contact point
      hip    hinge at z = 0.55       torso rotates relative to the shank

  Positive angle pitches the machine FORWARD (+x). Upright is qpos = [0, 0].

  Generalised coordinates (model.nq == 2):
      0  ankle   shank angle from vertical  [rad]
      1  hip     torso angle from the shank [rad]

  Actuators (model.nu == 2), both direct torque motors, gear 1, so data.ctrl[i] is a
  TORQUE IN NEWTON-METRES, not a target angle:
      0  ankle_torque   ctrlrange -40 40   the strong joint, and still the one that saturates
      1  hip_torque     ctrlrange -25 25

  The ctrlrange values are the point of the actuator-saturation section. Holding this body
  against gravity at a lean of theta costs roughly m*g*h*sin(theta) at the ankle, and with
  m = 20 kg and the centre of mass near 0.68 m that is about 133 N*m per radian. A 40 N*m
  ankle therefore recovers a small lean comfortably and cannot recover a large one, however
  large a gain the controller asks for. MuJoCo clamps the request; the controller does not
  get to know it was clamped unless it looks.

  Masses are stated explicitly rather than derived from a density, so that every number in
  the lesson's output traces back to something legible in this file.
-->
<mujoco model="f15l08_balancer">
  <compiler angle="radian" autolimits="true"/>

  <option timestep="0.004" integrator="implicitfast" gravity="0 0 -9.81">
    <!-- Nothing in this task touches anything. A contact-free step is the cheapest honest
         unit of work for a study that needs thousands of rollouts. -->
    <flag contact="disable"/>
  </option>

  <visual>
    <rgba haze="0.15 0.25 0.35 1"/>
    <global elevation="-15" azimuth="90"/>
  </visual>

  <default>
    <geom type="capsule" contype="0" conaffinity="0" rgba="0.72 0.74 0.80 1"/>
    <joint type="hinge" axis="0 1 0" armature="0.01"/>
    <motor gear="1" ctrllimited="true"/>
  </default>

  <worldbody>
    <light pos="0 0 3" dir="0 0 -1" diffuse="0.8 0.8 0.8"/>
    <geom name="floor" type="plane" size="5 5 0.1" pos="0 0 0" rgba="0.26 0.28 0.33 1"
          contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>

    <body name="shank" pos="0 0 0">
      <joint name="ankle" damping="0.30" range="-1.4 1.4"/>
      <geom name="shank_geom" fromto="0 0 0 0 0 0.55" size="0.05" mass="6"/>

      <body name="torso" pos="0 0 0.55">
        <joint name="hip" damping="0.30" range="-1.0 1.0"/>
        <geom name="torso_geom" fromto="0 0 0 0 0 0.60" size="0.07" mass="14"
              rgba="0.62 0.66 0.74 1"/>
        <site name="head" pos="0 0 0.60" size="0.02"/>
      </body>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <actuator>
    <motor name="ankle_torque" joint="ankle" ctrlrange="-40 40"/>
    <motor name="hip_torque"   joint="hip"   ctrlrange="-25 25"/>
  </actuator>

  <keyframe>
    <!-- The disturbance the controller is asked to reject: a forward lean of 0.15 rad with
         0.5 rad/s of forward angular velocity already on the body.

         This number is calibrated, not decorative. Holding the body statically at a lean of
         theta costs about m*g*h*sin(theta) at the ankle, which for this model is roughly
         133*sin(theta) N*m, so a 40 N*m ankle stalls at about 0.31 rad. Starting at 0.15 rad
         and still falling forwards puts the recovery squarely in the region where the torque
         limit is a live constraint: a good controller recovers with the saturation indicator
         already lit, and a 20% heavier torso does not recover at all.

         That margin is the whole lesson. Start much smaller and every reality gap is
         survivable, so the student measures nothing; start much larger and nothing survives,
         so the student measures nothing. -->
    <key name="lean" qpos="0.15 -0.05" qvel="0.50 0.0"/>
  </keyframe>
</mujoco>
